# Test conventions

<!--
This is a portable set of test-discipline rules — nothing here is stack-specific.
Move (do not copy) this file to your actual test tree's root once one exists
(e.g. `frontend/tests/CLAUDE.md`, `e2e/CLAUDE.md`) and rename it to `CLAUDE.md`,
so it loads automatically whenever Claude reads or edits a file under there.
Blueprint itself ships no application test tree, so this stays a `.example`
file at the repository root until a downstream project creates one.
-->

## Always

- Tests are part of the feature commit — never defer them to a follow-up
- Stale mocks fail silently: if a module's exported API changes, every test that mocks it must be updated in the same commit
- Run the affected test file locally before every `git commit` — CI is a safety net, not the first line of defense
- Integration tests should hit real implementations, not mocks of internal modules
- Cover rollback/failure paths and empty states, not just the happy path

## A green suite is not the same as a working suite

The failure mode that costs the most is not a failing test — it is a test that
silently never runs, or runs and asserts nothing. Guard against all three:

- **A test file that is never collected.** A path filter, a renamed directory, or
  a config `exclude` can drop whole directories from the CI run with nothing
  failing. Assert that every test file on disk is collected by the exact command
  CI invokes, and run that check early, independent of the slow shards.
- **A new file that never enters the coverage report.** If coverage
  instrumentation is scoped to imported modules, a brand-new untested file is
  invisible to both diff-coverage and the package floor — it is not uncovered, it
  is *absent*. Fail when a file added in the change is missing from the coverage
  report entirely.
- **A test that executes a line without checking it.** Coverage answers "did this
  run?", not "would this fail if it were wrong?". Use property-based tests for
  invariants, and mutation testing on core logic to find the assertions you are
  missing.

## Watch the test fail first — a test that cannot fail is not coverage

The most expensive test is not one that fails; it is one that **passes on the
unfixed build**. It ships as evidence, it is counted in coverage, and it will
never object again. Every one of these was written in good faith and reviewed:

- **An absence assertion that samples too early.** Polling a counter for `0` to
  prove a write was refused matches on the *first* sample — before the action
  could have fired — and passes identically with the guard deleted. Assert on a
  request that must be *rejected*, or on a synchronous observable, not on the
  continued absence of something slow.
- **A key sequence that cancels itself.** A guard test pressing `↓ ↓ ↑` passes
  with the guard removed: two fixture rows clamp the second `↓`, and the `↑`
  undoes the first. Drive **one** action from a known starting state, and assert
  the fixture is large enough for the movement to be observable.
- **A selector that binds to the wrong node.** Accessible-name matching is a
  *substring* match by default, so a query for one region silently binds to an
  empty-state region whose label merely starts the same — and the "reproduction"
  then passes against the broken build. Use exact matching for names that share
  a prefix.
- **A parameterized guard whose parameters do not vary.** A query-count check at
  two page sizes is vacuous when the framework ignores the page-size parameter:
  it issues two identical requests and compares them. Assert an absolute budget
  and prove it fails when the optimization is removed.

The rule that catches all four costs one command:

> **Revert the fix, run the new test, and require it to FAIL. Then reapply.**
> If you cannot revert cleanly, break the production code deliberately — comment
> out the guard, return the wrong value — and watch it go red.

Record it in the MR: "verified failing without the fix". A test whose red state
nobody has ever seen is an assertion about the author's intent, not about the code.

## Never truncate a mandatory sweep

Piping a required audit through `head` — the E2E grep sweep, a coverage
enumeration, a rename check — makes an **incomplete** answer look complete.
Running the rule and then discarding most of its output feels like having
checked. Use `grep -rl` plus per-file counts when the raw output is large, and
report the total.

Related: shells differ on word splitting. In `zsh`, an unquoted `$SUITES`
variable does **not** split into arguments, so a batched `pytest $SUITES` gets
one bogus argument, collects nothing, and the empty result reads as
**0 failures = pass**. Before believing any zero, confirm the run reported a
non-zero *total*.

## Hermeticity — a test that reaches the network is not a test

- Ban outbound sockets in the test suite by default. Derive the allowlist from
  configuration (database host, cache host) rather than hardcoding it, so it is
  identical in CI and locally, and resolve each host to its IP addresses — socket
  guards match the address actually passed to `connect()`, not the hostname.
- A test that genuinely needs the network opts out with an explicit, reviewable
  marker. Everything else fails instantly instead of hanging on a connect timeout
  and flaking by test order.

## A flake is a bug until proven otherwise

Treat an intermittent failure as a defect report, not as noise to be retried
away. In practice most "flaky" tests are real bugs in the product or the harness
— a race a user can also hit, a listener attached to an element that does not
exist yet, a component that crashes on a malformed response.

- Allow **at most one** retry in CI and **zero** locally. One retry absorbs a
  genuine runner blip; two lets a test that fails two of three attempts report
  success.
- Fail a dedicated job on any test that passed *only* on retry, so a retry can
  never mask a defect silently.
- Emulate reduced motion suite-wide for browser tests. A looping loading skeleton
  keeps layout permanently in motion and defeats the runner's element-stability
  check, surfacing as an inexplicable timeout on an unrelated control.
- Test the **cold** path deliberately. If every endpoint resolves instantly,
  whether a component renders its loading state first is left to chance — so the
  path real users get is only covered by accident. Add a helper that delays a
  route and assert the surface still works when its container mounts after first
  paint.

## Browser/E2E mocks

- **Register a catch-all route first.** Any endpoint the spec forgot to mock
  otherwise escapes to whatever is listening — nothing in CI, a real backend on a
  developer machine — so the same spec behaves differently per environment. Make
  the catch-all answer with a typed error plus a console warning naming the URL,
  so a missing mock becomes a deterministic warning rather than a race.
- **A catch-all does not mean the page is mocked.** A generic list-shaped stub
  returned for an object-shaped endpoint produces a truthy but malformed value,
  the component throws, and an error boundary replaces the app — which surfaces
  later as an unrelated timeout, not as "missing mock". Mock every endpoint the
  page actually reads, with its real response shape.
- **Gate on a "page rendered" signal** before interacting with a data-driven
  route — a heading or content node that only appears once those reads resolve —
  not just the control's own visibility.
- **Validate every mock payload against the API schema, at test time.** A
  hand-written fixture drifts from the server silently, and the spec keeps
  passing against a shape production never sends. Install one guard in the shared
  catch-all setup that checks each fulfilled response against the schema the API
  declares for that method, path, and status, and throw inside the route handler
  so the spec fails by name. Upstream a single install line reached ~290 specs
  whose payloads were inlined in the spec body; a lint rule stops that line being
  quietly deleted. Enforce an unknown property, a wrong type, an impossible enum,
  and a null on a non-nullable field. Do **not** enforce missing-required: a
  fixture sending `{id, name}` is an intentional minimal stand-in. When the guard
  fires, decide which artifact is wrong before "fixing" the mock — upstream, five
  flagged endpoints returned a bare array while the *schema* wrongly declared
  pagination. An operation whose body is declared free-form `{type: object}` is a
  hole in the schema, not coverage; count those out loud.
- **A spec that asserts after a write needs a stateful mock.** The app patches its
  cache optimistically, then invalidates and refetches on success. A stateless
  list mock re-serves the original rows, so the refetch **erases the committed
  value**, which then exists only for the milliseconds between the optimistic
  render and the refetch landing. On a loaded CI runner the assertion misses that
  window: green on the branch, red on the default branch. **Raising the timeout or
  the retry count fixes nothing** — after the refetch the state is gone for good,
  so a 5 s and a 60 s wait fail identically. Serve reads from a live row array the
  write handler mutates. Reproduce this class with
  `--repeat-each=15 --workers=<2–3× cores>`; a default local run will not show it.
- **Each worktree needs its own E2E server port.** Playwright's
  `reuseExistingServer` identifies "an existing server" by **port alone**, so with
  a hardcoded port every worktree on the machine shares one preview server, and a
  local run asserts against whichever checkout started it. The dangerous direction
  is the one that **passes**: green against a sibling's bundle looks exactly like a
  trustworthy result. `scripts/wt new` exports `WT_E2E_PORT` / `WT_E2E_DEV_PORT`
  into each `.envrc`. Read them in one place, feed a single `PORT` constant to
  `baseURL`, `webServer.url`, **and** the server's `--port` flag so the three
  cannot disagree, pass `--strictPort` so a bind mismatch fails loudly, and throw
  on a present-but-invalid value rather than falling back to the default — a typo
  that silently reverts to the shared port reintroduces the collision while
  looking configured. Unset (main checkout, CI) the defaults apply unchanged.

## Before committing a user-visible change, grep the E2E specs

E2E specs live in a separate tree from the source and assert on text, ARIA roles,
menu items, section counts, and key bindings. Unit tests sit next to the
component and are obvious to update; E2E specs are not, which is why they are the
most common CI failure. Before committing any UI surface change:

```bash
grep -rn "<old text or label>"        <e2e dir>/   # string assertions
grep -rn "<menu / dialog / aria-label>" <e2e dir>/   # role + accessible name
grep -rn "press('<old key>')"          <e2e dir>/   # keyboard rebinds
```

Update every match in the **same commit**, and run the affected spec locally.
Added or removed a list item, section, or menu option? Search for the count and
the sibling names too — an assertion like "all five sections" fails the moment
you add a sixth.
